content.text:
Here is a medium hard SQL interview prompt for analytics candidates.

Scenario: A retail operations team needs a monthly category performance report. You have tables: accounts(account_uid, joined_on, nation, tier); orders(order_uid, account_uid, placed_on, status); line_items(line_id, order_uid, sku, qty, list_price, promo_pct); catalog(sku, department, vendor, launched_on, unit_margin); settlements(settlement_id, order_uid, paid_on, gross, currency); refunds(refund_id, order_uid, refunded_on, cause); tickets(ticket_id, order_uid, opened_on, agent, state); loyalty(member_id, account_uid, points, earned_on); promotions(promo_id, sku, started_on, ended_on, code); inventory(warehouse_id, sku, units, updated_on).

For each calendar month, nation, and department, calculate:
one net revenue, meaning settlements gross minus refunds for paid orders;
two paid order count, distinct order_uid values with positive settlement;
three average discounted margin, approximated by qty times list_price times one minus promo_pct divided by one hundred, then multiplied by unit_margin;
four repeat customer count, accounts placing at least two paid orders that month;
five refund ratio, refunds divided by gross, guarded against zero;
six top department rank, using dense_rank partitioned by month;
seven latest ticket state, showing the most recent support outcome;
eight loyalty points delta, points earned during the month.

Assume UTC timestamps, missing promo_pct means zero, cancelled orders excluded, multiple settlements allowed, refunds may exceed gross, and all values are USD. Provide one SQL query using common table expressions, window functions, date_trunc, coalesce, and lateral joins if helpful. State assumptions and describe how you would validate results using synthetic fixtures, boundary dates, ties, missing data, and performance indexing.

Deliver one concise answer. It should include readable aliases, deterministic ordering, clear comments, and an indexing note. The interviewer may ask follow ups about null safety, tie handling, timezone conversion, incremental maintenance, and cost estimation.

